    overview: 'Acute heart failure (AHF) is a rapid onset or worsening of heart failure symptoms requiring urgent evaluation and treatment, commonly presenting as acute pulmonary oedema (warm and wet profile), cardiogenic shock (cold and wet), or low-output failure (cold and dry). The pathophysiology involves acute decompensation of chronic heart failure precipitated by myocardial ischaemia, medication non-adherence, dietary indiscretion, arrhythmias, or intercurrent infection, leading to elevated left ventricular filling pressures and pulmonary congestion. Immediate management centres on IV loop diuretics (furosemide 20-80 mg IV bolus or 5-40 mg/hr infusion, doubling the patient\'s oral dose), IV vasodilators (nitroglycerin 10-200 mcg/min or nitroprusside) for afterload reduction in hypertensive AHF, and inotropic support (dobutamine 2.5-20 mcg/kg/min or milrinone) only in cardiogenic shock with evidence of hypoperfusion. Non-invasive ventilation (CPAP/BiPAP) is first-line for acute pulmonary oedema with respiratory distress and reduces intubation rates. Identify and treat the precipitating factor, and transition to optimised GDMT (ACEi/ARNI + beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i) once haemodynamically stable.',
