    monitoring: 'Fasting lipid panel (TC, LDL-C, HDL-C, TG) at baseline, 4-12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then annually. ALT/AST at baseline, at 3 months if baseline elevated, then as clinically indicated. CK only if patient develops muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness (do not routinely monitor). HbA1c at baseline and annually (statins slightly increase diabetes risk). ASCVD 10-year risk score recalculated periodically. Hepatic function before starting ezetimibe. Renal function for fenofibrate dosing. Assess medication adherence and lifestyle modifications at each visit.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'What is the LDL-C target for a patient with established coronary artery disease (very high risk)?', options: ['A. <3.0 mmol/L', 'B. <2.6 mmol/L', 'C. <1.8 mmol/L', 'D. <1.4 mmol/L'], correctAnswer: 'D', explanation: 'Very high-risk patients (established ASCVD, recurrent events, or ASCVD with diabetes) should achieve LDL-C <1.4 mmol/L (<55 mg/dL). This requires high-intensity statin Â± ezetimibe Â± PCSK9 inhibitor. The 2019 ESC/EAS guidelines recommend this aggressive target based on meta-analysis data showing each 1 mmol/L reduction in LDL-C reduces major cardiovascular events by 22%.' },
      { question: 'A patient on atorvastatin 80 mg daily develops severe myalgia with CK 8Ã— ULN. What is the appropriate management?', options: ['A. Reduce atorvastatin to 40 mg', 'B. Switch to rosuvastatin 20 mg', 'C. Stop statin and check thyroid function', 'D. Add coenzyme Q10 and continue'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'CK >4Ã— ULN with symptoms indicates definite statin-associated muscle symptoms (SAMS). The statin should be discontinued, thyroid function checked (hypothyroidism potentiates myopathy), and renal function assessed. Once resolved, try a lower dose of a different statin, alternate-day dosing, or switch to a non-statin (ezetimibe, PCSK9i). Coenzyme Q10 has limited evidence.' },
      { question: 'For a patient with mixed hyperlipidaemia (LDL-C 3.5 mmol/L, TG 4.2 mmol/L) on maxstatin, what is the best add-on therapy?', options: ['A. Niacin', 'B. Fenofibrate', 'C. Ezetimibe', 'D. Bile acid sequestrant'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Fenofibrate is a PPAR-alpha agonist that reduces triglycerides by 30-50% and modestly raises HDL-C. It is the preferred add-on for mixed hyperlipidaemia with elevated TG. Ezetimibe primarily lowers LDL-C and has minimal TG effect. Niacin has fallen out of favour (AIM-HIGH, HPS2-THRIVE trials). Bile acid sequestrants can worsen TG levels.' },
      { question: 'How much does high-intensity statin therapy reduce LDL-C?', options: ['A. 10-20%', 'B. 30-40%', 'C. 50-60%', 'D. 70-80%'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'High-intensity statins (atorvastatin 40-80 mg, rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) reduce LDL-C by approximately 50-60%. Moderate-intensity statins reduce by 30-40%, and low-intensity by <30%. This is based on dose-response data from the CTT meta-analysis of >170,000 participants.' },
      { question: 'At what LDL-C level should familial hypercholesterolaemia (FH) be suspected in an adult without secondary causes?', options: ['A. >2.6 mmol/L', 'B. >3.5 mmol/L', 'C. >4.9 mmol/L', 'D. >6.5 mmol/L'], correctAnswer: 'C', explanation: 'FH should be suspected if LDL-C >4.9 mmol/L (>190 mg/dL) in adults without secondary causes (nephrotic syndrome, hypothyroidism, cholestasis). Homozygous FH presents with LDL-C >13 mmol/L. The Dutch Lipid Clinic Network criteria or Simon Broome criteria are used for diagnosis. Cascade screening of first-degree relatives is essential.' },
    ],
