    unitId: 'cp-gi',
    specialty: 'Gastrointestinal Disorders',
    overview: 'Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease: chronic symptoms or mucosal damage caused by abnormal reflux of gastric contents (acid, pepsin, bile) into the oesophagus through a dysfunctional lower oesophageal sphincter (LOS). Typical symptoms: heartburn (retrosternal burning), regurgitation, dysphagia. Complications: erosive oesophagitis (Los Angeles classification A-D), Barrett oesophagus (intestinal metaplasia â€” risk of oesophageal adenocarcinoma), oesophageal stricture. Lifestyle modifications: weight loss, elevate head of bed 15-20cm, avoid trigger foods (fatty food, caffeine, alcohol, chocolate, mint), avoid eating 2-3 hours before bed, smoking cessation. Mild/intermittent: antacids or H2RA. Moderate-severe or erosive: PPI (omeprazole 20mg daily for 4-8 weeks, then step down). Barrett oesophagus: surveillance endoscopy every 3-5 years.',
