    monitoring: 'GAD-7 score q2-4 weeks (target â‰¥50% reduction or remission <5). Panic attack frequency diary (weekly during titration). Side effect review at 2 weeks (initial worsening may occur). Suicidal ideation screening (especially age <25). Benzodiazepine review q2 weeks â€” enforce 4-week maximum. Adherence assessment. CBT engagement (if combined therapy). Functioning (WHODAS or SDS). Substance use screening. Consider venlafaxine or pregabalin if SSRI inadequate at 6-8 weeks.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for moderate-severe GAD?', options: ['A. Diazepam', 'B. Sertraline (SSRI/SNRI)', 'C. Propranolol', 'D. Hydroxyzine'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'SSRIs (sertraline, escitalopram) and SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine) are first-line for GAD based on efficacy and safety. They take 2-4 weeks for full effect. Benzodiazepines are second-line and limited to 2-4 weeks maximum due to tolerance and dependence. Pregabalin is a NICE-recommended alternative.' },
      { question: 'What is the maximum recommended duration for benzodiazepine prescription in anxiety disorders?', options: ['A. 1 week', 'B. 2-4 weeks', 'C. 3 months', 'D. 6 months'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Benzodiazepines should not be prescribed for >2-4 weeks due to rapid tolerance, physical dependence, and withdrawal syndrome. UK BNF and NICE guidelines recommend short-term use only (2-4 weeks, ideally 1-2 weeks). Withdrawal should be gradual (reduce by 10-25% every 1-2 weeks) to avoid seizures and rebound anxiety.' },
      { question: 'A patient presents with palpitations, chest tightness, dyspnoea, and fear of dying, resolving in 10 minutes. ECG and troponin are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?', options: ['A. Acute coronary syndrome', 'B. Panic attack', 'C. Hyperthyroidism', 'D. Pulmonary embolism'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Recurrent episodes of discrete surges of fear/ discomfort with physical symptoms (palpitations, chest pain, dyspnoea, dizziness, derealisation) peaking within 10 minutes and resolving spontaneously are characteristic of panic attacks. Rule out organic causes (ECG, thyroid, glucose). Treatment: CBT + SSRI; benzodiazepines only for acute episodes.' },
    ],
