    unitId: 'cp-neuro',
    specialty: 'Neurological Disorders',
    overview: 'Migraine is a primary headache disorder characterised by recurrent moderate-to-severe attacks lasting 4-72 hours, typically unilateral, throbbing, aggravated by activity, and associated with nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia. About 30% experience a preceding aura (visual field defects, scintillating scotoma, sensory paraesthesia, dysphasia) lasting 5-60 minutes. Pathophysiology involves cortical spreading depression (aura), trigeminal activation, and CGRP-mediated neurogenic inflammation. Acute treatment: simple analgesics (aspirin 900 mg, ibuprofen 400 mg) for mild attacks; triptans (sumatriptan 50-100 mg PO or 6 mg SC) for moderate-severe attacks, taken early. Prophylaxis indicated if â‰¥4 attacks/month or significant disability: propranolol 80-160 mg/day, amitriptyline 25-75 mg at night, topiramate 25-100 mg BD, or CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, fremanezumab) for refractory cases.',
    diagram: MIGRAINE_DIAGRAM,
    keyDrugs: [
      { drug: 'Sumatriptan 50-100 mg PO at onset (max 300 mg/day); 6 mg SC if PO fails (max 12 mg/day)', class: 'Triptan (5-HT1B/1D agonist)', sideEffects: ['Chest tightness/pressure (10%, transient, rarely ischaemic)', 'Paraesthesia (tingling in face and limbs)', 'Fatigue and heaviness', 'Nausea (may need antiemetic)', 'Coronary vasospasm â€” contraindicated in CAD, uncontrolled HTN, stroke, PVD, hemiplegic migraine', 'Serotonin syndrome (if combined with SSRIs â€” rare)', 'Rebound headache with overuse (>10 days/month)'] },
      { drug: 'Propranolol 80-160 mg/day BD-OD (start 40 mg BD, titrate q2-4wk)', class: 'Beta-blocker (preventive)', sideEffects: ['Bradycardia and hypotension', 'Fatigue and lethargy', 'Cold extremities (peripheral vasoconstriction)', 'Bronchospasm (contraindicated in asthma)', 'Sleep disturbance and nightmares', 'Sexual dysfunction', 'Masking of hypoglycaemia (diabetes)'] },
      { drug: 'Topiramate 25 mg ON, titrate by 25 mg q1-2wk (target 50-100 mg BD)', class: 'Anticonvulsant (preventive)', sideEffects: ['Paraesthesia (dose-dependent, 30%)', 'Cognitive dulling ("word-finding difficulty", 20%)', 'Weight loss (appetite suppression)', 'Nephrolithiasis (2-3%, avoid in kidney stones)', 'Closed-angle glaucoma (rare, ophthalmology referral)', 'Metabolic acidosis (bicarbonate monitoring)', 'Oligohydramnios (teratogenic â€” avoid in pregnancy)'] },
    ],
    monitoring: 'Headache diary: frequency, severity (1-10 VAS), duration, triggers, acute medication use, and response â€” review q3mo. MIDAS (Migraine Disability Assessment) or HIT-6 score at baseline and q6mo (target â‰¥50% reduction). BP and HR q1-3 months (propranolol). Renal function and bicarbonate q6mo (topiramate â€” nephrolithiasis, metabolic acidosis). Acute medication use: limit triptans to <10 days/month, simple analgesics <15 days/month to prevent medication-overuse headache. Weight monitoring (topiramate â€” weight loss; propranolol â€” weight neutral). Sleep quality. Contraception (topiramate teratogenic). Ophthalmology referral if visual symptoms or topiramate use.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to triptan use?', options: ['A. Nausea', 'B. Uncontrolled hypertension and history of ischaemic stroke', 'C. Photophobia', 'D. Age >50 years'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists that cause coronary and systemic vasoconstriction. Absolute contraindications: uncontrolled hypertension, ischaemic heart disease, Prinzmetal angina, PVD, cerebrovascular disease (stroke/TIA), hemiplegic migraine, and within 24h of ergotamine. NICE recommends triptans only after simple analgesics have failed.' },
      { question: 'A patient has â‰¥4 migraine attacks per month with significant disability despite lifestyle modification. What is the most appropriate next step?', options: ['A. Increase sumatriptan dose', 'B. Start migraine prophylaxis (propranolol or topiramate)', 'C. CT brain', 'D. Refer for occipital nerve block'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Prophylaxis is indicated for â‰¥4 attacks/month, significant disability, or contraindication/failure of acute treatment. First-line: propranolol (80-160 mg/day) or topiramate (25-100 mg BD). NICE recommends CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab 140 mg SC monthly) for â‰¥4 attacks refractory to â‰¥2 prophylactics. Review efficacy after 3 months.' },
      { question: 'What is the most common adverse effect of topiramate used for migraine prophylaxis?', options: ['A. Weight gain', 'B. Paraesthesia and cognitive dulling', 'C. Hepatotoxicity', 'D. Hair loss'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Paraesthesia (tingling in fingers/toes) affects ~30% of patients on topiramate and is dose-dependent. Cognitive dulling (word-finding difficulty, impaired concentration) occurs in ~20%. Both are usually mild and settle with dose adjustment. Weight loss (appetite suppression) is common and may be beneficial or distressing.' },
      { question: 'A migraine patient uses sumatriptan 3 times per week. What complication should be warned about?', options: ['A. Hepatotoxicity', 'B. Medication-overuse headache', 'C. Rebound hypertension', 'D. Serotonin syndrome'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Triptan overuse (>10 days/month) causes medication-overuse headache (MOH) â€” a chronic daily or near-daily headache maintained by the analgesic itself. Management: abrupt withdrawal of the overused medication (with bridge therapy if needed), headache diary, and switch to prophylaxis. Sumatriptan should be limited to â‰¤2 days/week.' },
      { question: 'A patient reports a 30-minute visual disturbance of fortification spectra followed by unilateral throbbing headache. What is the most likely diagnosis?', options: ['A. Tension headache', 'B. Migraine with aura', 'C. Cluster headache', 'D. Subarachnoid haemorrhage'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Migraine with aura presents with transient neurological symptoms (visual most common â€” scintillating scotoma, fortification spectra, hemianopia) lasting 5-60 minutes, followed by headache. The visual aura reflects cortical spreading depression. Cluster headache causes severe unilateral orbital pain with autonomic symptoms (lacrimation, rhinorrhoea) and occurs in clusters.' },
    ],
