    overview: 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis is an acid-fast bacillus transmitted via aerosol that targets alveolar macrophages, forming granulomatous tubercles. Pulmonary TB is most common, presenting with chronic cough >2 weeks, haemoptysis, night sweats, weight loss, and low-grade fever. Diagnosis requires sputum AFB smear/microscopy, GeneXpert MTB/RIF, culture, and chest X-ray showing upper lobe infiltrates or cavitation. First-line treatment follows the WHO RIPE regimen: Rifampicin 600mg, Isoniazid 300mg, Pyrazinamide 1500mg, and Ethambutol 1500mg daily for 2 months (intensive phase), followed by Rifampicin 600mg + Isoniazid 300mg daily for 4 months (continuation phase), totalling 6 months under DOTS. Kenya is a high-burden country; all patients with cough >2 weeks must be screened. MDR-TB requires fluoroquinolone-based regimens with second-line agents for 9-20 months.',
