    monitoring: 'FBC with differential and blast count: at diagnosis, then weekly during induction and consolidation (monitor for marrow recovery â€” ANC >0.5 Ã— 10â¹/L indicates neutrophil recovery). Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy: at day 14-21 post-induction (early response), then at end of induction for complete remission assessment (blasts <5%). Cytogenetics and molecular markers: at diagnosis and relapse (FLT3-ITD â†’ midostaurin; NPM1 â†’ prognosis). Tumour lysis syndrome (TLS) prophylaxis: aggressive IV fluids (3L/mÂ²/day), allopurinol 100mg TID (or rasburicase 0.2mg/kg if high risk: WBC >100 Ã— 10â¹/L, high LDH, high uric acid). Monitor: Kâº, POâ‚„, CaÂ²âº, uric acid, creatinine, LDH every 6-8h for 48-72h post-chemotherapy. ECG: before and during anthracycline therapy (QTc, arrhythmia monitoring). LVEF: baseline and after each anthracycline cycle. Hepatic and renal function: weekly during intensive therapy. Infectious screening and prophylaxis: antifungal (fluconazole), antiviral (acyclovir), anti-PJP (co-trimoxazole after remission). Nutritional status: high-calorie diet, TPN if needed. For CLL: lymphocyte count monthly (ibrutinib response), LDH, Î²2-microglobulin, immunoglobulin levels, CT for lymphadenopathy assessment every 3-6 months.',
    mcqs: [
      { question: 'What is the "7+3" induction regimen for AML?', options: ['A. 7 days of cytarabine + 3 days of etoposide', 'B. 7 days of continuous cytarabine infusion + 3 days of daunorubicin', 'C. 7 days of daunorubicin + 3 days of cytarabine', 'D. 7 cycles of chemotherapy + 3 cycles of maintenance'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'The "7+3" regimen consists of: cytarabine 100-200mg/mÂ² as continuous IV infusion for 7 days + daunorubicin 60mg/mÂ² IV push for 3 days (days 1-3). This is the standard induction chemotherapy for AML in patients <60-65 years. Goal is complete remission (bone marrow blasts <5%, ANC >1 Ã— 10â¹/L, platelets >100 Ã— 10â¹/L).' },
      { question: 'Which cytogenetic abnormality in AML carries a favourable prognosis?', options: ['A. Complex karyotype (â‰¥3 abnormalities)', 'B. t(15;17) PML-RARA (APL)', 'C. Deletion 5q', 'D. Monosomy 7'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Favourable-risk cytogenetic abnormalities in AML include: t(8;21) RUNX1-RUNX1T1, inv(16) CBFB-MYH11, and t(15;17) PML-RARA (acute promyelocytic leukaemia â€” APL). APL is treated with ATRA + arsenic trioxide (ATRA-ATO) with >90% cure rate. Adverse risk: complex karyotype, monosomy 5/7, del(5q), del(7q), TP53 mutation.' },
      { question: 'What is the primary purpose of tumour lysis syndrome prophylaxis in leukaemia induction?', options: ['A. Prevent infection', 'B. Prevent hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, and hyperuricaemia from rapid cell lysis', 'C. Prevent bleeding', 'D. Prevent cardiotoxicity'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'TLS prophylaxis prevents life-threatening metabolic complications from massive tumour cell lysis: hyperkalaemia (cardiac arrest), hyperphosphataemia (renal failure, hypocalcaemia), hyperuricaemia (renal failure), and lactic acidosis. Prophylaxis: aggressive IV fluids, allopurinol (reduces uric acid production), rasburicase (converts uric acid to soluble allantoin). Monitor Kâº, POâ‚„, CaÂ²âº, uric acid, Cr q6-8h.' },
      { question: 'What is the first-line targeted therapy for CLL requiring treatment?', options: ['A. Imatinib', 'B. Ibrutinib (BTK inhibitor) 420mg PO daily', 'C. Gefitinib', 'D. Tamoxifen'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Ibrutinib (ibrutinib 420mg PO daily) is a first-in-class BTK (Bruton tyrosine kinase) inhibitor and is first-line for CLL requiring treatment (Rai stage III-IV, or symptomatic). It inhibits B-cell receptor signalling, causing CLL cell apoptosis. Superior to chemoimmunotherapy in older/unfit patients. Alternative: venetoclax (BCL2 inhibitor) + rituximab (fixed-duration). Watch and wait for early-stage asymptomatic CLL.' },
      { question: 'Which complication of CLL is characterised by transformation to aggressive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma?', options: ['A. Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia', 'B. Richter transformation', 'C. Prolymphocytic transformation', 'D. Tumour lysis syndrome'], correctAnswer: 'B', explanation: 'Richter transformation occurs in 5-10% of CLL patients â€” transformation from indolent CLL to aggressive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL). Presents with rapid lymph node enlargement, B symptoms, rising LDH, and deteriorating performance status. Median survival after Richter transformation is 8-12 months. Check TP53 status and consider chemo-immunotherapy or clinical trials.' },
    ],
