    specialty: 'Paediatric Pharmacotherapy',
    overview: 'Childhood pneumonia is the leading infectious cause of death in children under 5 years worldwide, caused predominantly by Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and respiratory syncytial virus. Pathophysiology involves microbial colonisation of the lower airways leading to alveolar consolidation, impaired gas exchange, and systemic inflammatory response. WHO IMCI classifies pneumonia by clinical signs: fast breathing alone = non-severe pneumonia, chest indrawing = severe pneumonia, and inability to drink or convulsions = very severe disease. Diagnosis is clinical using WHO criteria (tachypnoea: >=60 breaths/min in <2mo, >=50 in 2-12mo, >=40 in 1-5y); CXR is reserved for severe or treatment-failing cases. Management includes oral amoxicillin 25-50mg/kg TDS for 5 days (non-severe), IM ampicillin 50mg/kg + gentamicin 7.5mg/kg once daily for severe, and IV ampicillin 50mg/kg 6-hourly + gentamicin 7.5mg/kg OD for very severe. Oxygen supplementation for SpO2 <90% (target >=92%). Prevention: PCV13 and Hib conjugate vaccines, exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months.',
