    overview: 'Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the pilosebaceous unit, driven by four pathogenic factors: follicular hyperkeratinisation, excess sebum production (androgen-mediated), Cutibacterium acnes colonisation, and inflammation. Clinical lesions range from open/closed comedones (mild) to papules, pustules, nodules, and cysts (moderate-severe). Mild acne is managed with topical agents: benzoyl peroxide (BPO) 2.5â€“5% gel (first-line, reduces C. acnes, no resistance), combined with a topical retinoid (adapalene 0.1% OD). Moderate acne adds oral antibiotics (doxycycline 100 mg OD for 3 months maximum, or lymecycline). Severe nodulocystic acne or treatment failure requires oral isotretinoin 0.5â€“1 mg/kg/day for 15â€“20 weeks (cumulative dose 120â€“150 mg/kg). Isotretinoin is highly teratogenic: mandatory pregnancy testing monthly, two forms of contraception, and iPLEDGE-equivalent programme. Long-term remission occurs in ~85% after one course.',
